{"verdict":"changes_requested","issues":[{"severity":"major","file":"src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx","line":1,"description":"No unit tests were added for the new live preview functionality. The diff modifies 30+ existing test files but only for import path changes (contentstackContext → ContentstackContext). Criteria (2) requires new features to have corresponding vitest unit tests mirroring the src/ path. A test should verify that ContentstackProvider propagates live preview updates to consumers — e.g., mock @contentstack/live-preview-utils, trigger onEntryChange callback, and assert that a child component using useContent re-renders with updated content.","suggestedFix":"Add a test file at src/context/__tests__/ContentstackContext.spec.tsx (or .test.tsx) that: (1) mocks @contentstack/live-preview-utils with vi.mock, (2) renders a test consumer component using useContent, (3) invokes the onEntryChange callback registered by the provider, and (4) asserts the consumer re-renders with the updated content value. Also test that initial render shows defaultContent correctly."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx","line":40,"description":"The onEntryChange callback does setContent((previousContent) => ({ ...previousContent })), which creates only a shallow copy. If the Contentstack live preview SDK mutates nested objects in place (which is its documented behavior), the shallow copy triggers a React re-render but all nested object references remain identical. This works for triggering re-render, but the approach is fragile — if any consumer memoizes on a nested content reference, it will not detect the change. The prescribed fix said 'calls the state setter on each edit callback' — the setter should receive the actually updated content, not just a shallow clone of the same object.","suggestedFix":"Verify that the Contentstack live preview SDK's onEntryChange callback provides the updated entry data as a parameter. If it does, use that data: setContent(updatedEntry). If the SDK mutates the existing object in place and provides no parameter, the shallow copy approach is acceptable but should be documented with a comment explaining why shallow copy is sufficient (SDK mutates in place, shallow copy forces React re-render, getValue reads mutated nested values)."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx","line":14,"description":"footer?: [any] uses untyped any without an explanatory comment, violating TypeScript strict compliance criteria (1). This was carried over from the old file but is now in newly written code.","suggestedFix":"Replace [any] with a proper type (e.g., [IContentstackFooter] if such an interface exists) or add an explanatory comment: // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any — footer type not yet defined in Contentstack schema."}],"summary":"The implementation correctly addresses the prescribed root cause: ContentstackContext.tsx replaces the old immutable useMemo with useState + useEffect initializing @contentstack/live-preview-utils, and onEntryChange calls the state setter to propagate updates to all 236+ useContent consumers. The fix is minimal and well-scoped — no over-engineering detected. However, two major issues prevent approval: (1) No unit tests were added for the new live preview feature, violating the test coverage requirement; (2) The shallow-copy-only approach in the onEntryChange callback may not reliably propagate nested content updates if the SDK provides updated data that should be used directly. A minor TypeScript strict issue (untyped any for footer) is also present. The import path migration from contentstackContext to ContentstackContext across 30+ files is clean and consistent."}
